    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Genoese seaman who had traded from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> coast, Columbus won the backing of Ferdinand and Isabella in 1492 for a westward route to Asia built on a badly underestimated size of the Earth.</li><li>His landfall in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a> on 12 October 1492 and three later voyages opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and the coasts of South and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, though he never clearly renounced his belief that he had reached the Far East.</li><li>As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> he waged war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a>, imposed tribute and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> and shipped enslaved people to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>; accused of tyranny, he was arrested in 1500, and historians still dispute how far the depopulation that followed was his doing.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Stranded on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> in 1504, Columbus kept the island's inhabitants supplying his men by predicting the lunar eclipse of 29 February, using Abraham Zacuto's astronomical charts.</p></aside>
    </section>

