      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1451</span><span class="rf-text">Born in the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> to Domenico Colombo, a wool weaver, and Susanna Fontanarossa</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1476</span><span class="rf-text">Shipwrecked off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>, Portugal, after his Genoese trading fleet is attacked, and settles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1492</span><span class="rf-text">Agrees the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulations-of-santa-fe">Capitulations of Santa Fe</a> with Ferdinand and Isabella in April, sails from Palos in August and reaches Guanahani in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a> in October</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1493</span><span class="rf-text">Returns to a hero's welcome with captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taínos</a>, then leaves Cádiz with 17 ships and nearly 1,500 men to found colonies</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1495</span><span class="rf-text">Rounds up about 1,500 Arawaks in a slave raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> and ships about 500 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, about two hundred of whom die on the way</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1498</span><span class="rf-text">On his third voyage sends boats ashore on the Paria Peninsula, the first recorded European landing on the mainland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1500</span><span class="rf-text">Arrested by Francisco de Bobadilla after charges of tyranny and brutality, and sent back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in chains</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1506</span><span class="rf-text">Dies in Valladolid while still pressing the Crown for the privileges promised him in 1492</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By scholarly consensus Christopher Columbus was born in the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> between 25 August and 31 October 1451, though authors over the centuries have claimed him for other countries. His father, Domenico Colombo, was a wool weaver in Genoa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savona">Savona</a> who also kept a cheese stand where the boy worked; his mother was Susanna Fontanarossa. He had three brothers, among them Bartholomew, later a cartographer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, and a sister. His native tongue was presumably Ligurian; in Spanish he was Cristóbal Colón. By his own account he went to sea at fourteen.</p><p>From 1473 he worked as a business agent for the Spinola, Centurione and Di Negro families, sailing to Chios and, in 1476, with a convoy to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> that probably touched <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galway">Galway</a>. Shipwrecked off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> that August, he settled in Lisbon, where he lived from 1477 to 1485, traded for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madeira">Madeira</a> and married Felipa Perestrello e Moniz, daughter of the donatary captain of Porto Santo. Their son Diego was born in 1479 or 1480. Between 1482 and 1485 he traded on the West African coast as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina">Elmina</a>. Largely self-educated, he learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Portuguese and Castilian and read Ptolemy, Pierre d'Ailly's Imago Mundi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>; the historian Edmund Morgan judged that his ideas were "characteristically simple and strong and sometimes wrong".</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Family in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/diego-columbus">Diego Columbus<span>Child</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the 1480s Columbus and his brother proposed reaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a> by sailing west. His figures rested on errors: following Ptolemy and Alfraganus's Arabic miles he took the Earth to be far smaller than it is, and he put <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> about 2,400 nautical miles west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>, against an actual 10,600. About 1484 the advisers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-portugal">John II of Portugal</a> rejected the plan on exactly those grounds, and a second approach in 1488 failed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a> returned from rounding the southern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><p>He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1485, where in 1487 he took as his mistress Beatriz Enríquez de Arana, mother of his son Fernando. He presented his plan to Queen Isabella on 1 May 1486; her committee, like the Portuguese, found the distance grossly underestimated, but the monarchs kept him on an allowance. After Ferdinand and Isabella took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> in January 1492, a council under Hernando de Talavera again found the scheme implausible. Columbus had left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> when the king's clerk Luis de Santángel persuaded the queen, and a royal guard brought him back. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulations-of-santa-fe">Capitulations of Santa Fe</a> of April 1492 promised him, if he succeeded, the rank of Admiral of the Ocean Sea, the viceroyalty and governorship of any lands he claimed, and a tenth of their revenues in perpetuity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Columbus left Palos on 3 August 1492 with the Santa María, the Pinta and the Niña, and on 12 October landed on an island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a> its people called Guanahani. He called its inhabitants indios, wrote in his journal that they "should be good and intelligent servants", and took captives to guide him to gold. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, where the Santa María ran aground and he left 39 men at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-navidad">La Navidad</a>, he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in March 1493 and presented kidnapped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taínos</a> at court. His printed letter spread the news through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>His second voyage left Cádiz in September 1493 with 17 ships and nearly 1,500 men. He found La Navidad burned and its men killed, and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-isabela">La Isabela</a>, where disease and famine had killed two-thirds of the settlers by the end of 1494. Men he sent under Pedro Margarit raped, enslaved and robbed the islanders; Columbus then made war on the Taíno for nine months until they agreed to pay tribute, introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a>, and in February 1495 rounded up about 1,500 Arawaks, shipping about 500 to Spain as slaves. On the third voyage, in 1498, his men landed on the Paria Peninsula, the first recorded European landing on the South American mainland. The fourth, from 1502, explored the coasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> in search of a strait to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Columbus's rule on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> drew complaints from settlers as well as from the colonised. On his return in 1498 he found Spaniards in rebellion and hanged at least one leader. Accusations of tyranny and incompetence reached the court, which sent Francisco de Bobadilla to investigate. Bobadilla took depositions from the Admiral's enemies, declared himself governor and reported that Columbus governed by torture and mutilation: a man who stole corn had his ears and nose cut off and was sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and the dismembered bodies of rebels were paraded through the streets. Columbus denied the charges, and historians have disputed the report's neutrality, given the Spaniards' anti-Italian sentiment and Bobadilla's wish for his post. In October 1500 he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in chains.</p><p>Later judgements divide sharply. The Spanish historian Consuelo Varela wrote that his government "was characterized by a form of tyranny"; Basil Davidson called him the "father of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>"; George Tinker and Mark Freedman argue that he began a cycle of "murder, violence, and slavery" that disease alone cannot explain. Kris Lane disputes the word <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> where the atrocities were not intended, William J. Connell warns against judging the 15th century by 20th-century morality, and others hold that some accounts were exaggerated as part of the Black Legend. A study of Spanish archives found that the gold quotas were imposed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">cacique</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guarionex">Guarionex</a>, and no record of hands cut off for failing to pay them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ferdinand ordered Columbus released after six weeks in jail; the monarchs restored his freedom and wealth and agreed to fund a fourth voyage, but named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-de-ovando">Nicolás de Ovando</a> governor of the Indies in his place. On that voyage, in 1502, he was refused entry to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> with a hurricane approaching; his own ships survived while 20 of the 30 ships of the outgoing treasure fleet were lost with 500 lives. In June 1503 his damaged ships were beached on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, where he and 230 men were stranded for a year while Ovando, who detested him, obstructed their rescue. He reached Sanlúcar on 7 November 1504.</p><p>He grew increasingly religious and, probably with his son Diego and the Carthusian monk Gaspar Gorricio, compiled a Book of Privileges (1502) setting out what the Crown owed him and a Book of Prophecies (1505) placing his voyages within Christian eschatology. Ill since his first return voyage with attacks then taken for gout, sometimes bedridden for months, he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> in 1505 and followed the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segovia">Segovia</a> and Valladolid to press his claim to a tenth of the riches of the new lands. The Crown, holding that his removal as governor released it from the contract, refused. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> on 20 May 1506, aged 54. His heirs pursued the claims in the pleitos colombinos, which ran in various forms until 1790.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Columbus's voyages are considered a turning point in human history and often cited as the beginning of the modern era. They brought lasting contact between the hemispheres; the indigenous cultures before them are called pre-Columbian, and the movement of people, plants, animals, diseases and ideas that followed is known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbian-exchange">Columbian exchange</a>. Exposed to Old World diseases, warfare and forced labour, the peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> collapsed: within three to six decades the surviving Arawaks numbered in the hundreds. Scholars weigh the causes differently, Noble David Cook stressing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> and Andrés Reséndez <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the mines, and Samuel Eliot Morison, his biographer, wrote that the policy Columbus began "resulted in complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>".</p><p>For a century after his death his reputation suffered from his failures as an administrator. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a> he became a founding figure for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, lending his name to Columbia, and later an ethnic hero for Italian, Irish and Jewish immigrants; the 1893 Chicago fair was named the World's Columbian Exposition, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> made 12 October its national day. From the 1990s a narrative of genocide and destruction came to compete with that of the heroic discoverer. Edmundo O'Gorman rejected the idea that he "discovered" America as a later legend, the Norse had reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> about 500 years earlier, and after 2020 many of his monuments were removed.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">On the globe, stop by stop</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/journeys?j=columbus">The Voyages of Christopher Columbus<span>Journey</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Christopher Columbus, a Genoese navigator in Spanish service, crossed the Atlantic four times between 1492 and 1504 and began the European colonisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, while maintaining that he had reached Asia. His governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, with its wars, tribute and slave raids, ended in his arrest in 1500, and his place in history has moved from heroic discoverer to a figure of sharp dispute.</em></p>

